WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.351
(4D Shapes Falling) 

00:00:07.351 --> 00:00:11.990
This is a video about
life in other dimensions.

00:00:11.990 --> 00:00:18.310
Flat lifeforms in 2D landscapes, psychedelic
entities with higher spatial geometry, even

00:00:18.310 --> 00:00:20.070
beings that can ignore time.

00:00:20.070 --> 00:00:25.420
While our 3D brains can typically only perceive
a limited number of dimensions, with the help

00:00:25.420 --> 00:00:30.920
of computer simulations and a bit of sci-fi
creativity, we can conceptualize some truly

00:00:30.920 --> 00:00:33.239
mind-blowing hypothetical realities.

00:00:33.239 --> 00:00:42.900
So, say goodbye to the 3D world you know,
and prepare for life in lower and higher dimensions…

00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:51.750
We’ll start by voyaging down a dimensional
layer, to a universe of 2D Life.

00:00:51.750 --> 00:00:57.489
The beings of this universe can only move
on two axes, with no conception of depth.

00:00:57.489 --> 00:01:01.680
If we were to stick our faces through the
flat plane of their world, the visual might

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:06.549
be frightening, as the two-dimensional lifeforms
would only be able to perceive infinitely

00:01:06.549 --> 00:01:12.470
thin slices of our three-dimensional bodies
at a time, a bit like the images of an MRI

00:01:12.470 --> 00:01:13.470
machine.

00:01:13.470 --> 00:01:18.181
To those beings, we would be shape-shifters
whose bodies appear from nowhere and disappear

00:01:18.181 --> 00:01:20.030
with no explanation.

00:01:20.030 --> 00:01:22.240
But could life really function in 2D?

00:01:22.240 --> 00:01:24.400
Sure, here’s some now.

00:01:24.400 --> 00:01:29.250
This is a simulation running in ALIEN, an
artificial life program that lets you build

00:01:29.250 --> 00:01:31.540
two-dimensional ecosystems.

00:01:31.540 --> 00:01:36.220
These virtual cells might not be life as we
typically define it, but they do self-replicate

00:01:36.220 --> 00:01:38.390
and adapt to environmental changes.

00:01:38.390 --> 00:01:43.800
There’s lots of impressive 2D life simulators
out there, but for 2D life to exist outside

00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:48.580
of a computer, it couldn’t be made of the
3D atoms we’re familiar with.

00:01:48.580 --> 00:01:53.579
Scientist Timm Hurton successfully simulated
a stable molecule in just two dimensions,

00:01:53.579 --> 00:01:58.690
so our hypothetical 2D organism could be made
of these bead-like components.

00:01:58.690 --> 00:02:02.530
One of the biggest hurdles for two-dimensional
life would likely be innards.

00:02:02.530 --> 00:02:07.220
Nearly all three-dimensional animals are built
around a digestive tract that passes through

00:02:07.220 --> 00:02:13.840
their bodies, with a hole on one end for eating,
and a hole on the other end for, uh, expelling.

00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:18.310
But if a two-dimensional lifeform had such
a system, it would cut the organism in half,

00:02:18.310 --> 00:02:23.629
as this ‘advanced simulation’ shows — with
the bifurcated segments of the lifeform falling

00:02:23.629 --> 00:02:24.690
apart.

00:02:24.690 --> 00:02:26.739
But there might be a solution.

00:02:26.739 --> 00:02:32.440
Certain animals like Sea Anemones just have
the one hole, for all their various hole-related

00:02:32.440 --> 00:02:33.440
needs.

00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:36.269
Which is… something I’m not going to think
too hard about.

00:02:36.269 --> 00:02:41.170
But perhaps our theoretical 2D lifeform could
have a similar system, with a single aperture

00:02:41.170 --> 00:02:43.850
leading to a simple digestive tract.

00:02:43.850 --> 00:02:49.110
Of course, it’s worth noting that just because
something could exist, doesn’t mean it does.

00:02:49.110 --> 00:02:54.530
As far as we’re aware, there is no secret
realm of 2D life: it’s just an interesting

00:02:54.530 --> 00:02:55.530
hypothetical.

00:02:55.530 --> 00:03:00.180
But what would happen if we went up a level,
to a universe of 4D life?

00:03:00.180 --> 00:03:05.060
The innovate game 4D Toys is a way for us
three dimensional lifeforms to start getting

00:03:05.060 --> 00:03:07.440
our minds around four-dimensional objects.

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:13.170
At first, these objects don’t seem to make
sense, changing shape as we move them and

00:03:13.170 --> 00:03:15.110
even disappearing from view entirely.

00:03:15.110 --> 00:03:16.140
What’s happening?

00:03:16.140 --> 00:03:22.709
To answer that, let’s return to our metaphor
of a 2D being observing 3D objects passing

00:03:22.709 --> 00:03:24.450
through its flat realm.

00:03:24.450 --> 00:03:29.989
From our perspective, these 3D objects are
straightforward, but to the 2D lifeform, they

00:03:29.989 --> 00:03:35.010
don’t follow familiar rules, appearing and
disappearing for seemingly no reason.

00:03:35.010 --> 00:03:40.180
As 3D beings, we can see the objects just
fell into the third dimension outside of the

00:03:40.180 --> 00:03:43.819
2D plane, but the 2D entity has no awareness
of this.

00:03:43.819 --> 00:03:47.640
And that’s exactly what’s happening to
us in 4D Toys.

00:03:47.640 --> 00:03:54.239
We’re three-dimensional beings that can
only see a limited, 3D slice of larger, fourth-dimensional

00:03:54.239 --> 00:03:55.239
objects.

00:03:55.239 --> 00:03:58.049
Take this ball pit filled with 200 4D hyperspheres.

00:03:58.049 --> 00:04:03.099
At first, it doesn’t seem like there’s
that many spheres on screen, but if we move

00:04:03.099 --> 00:04:07.739
our third dimensional slice through the fourth
dimension, just like you can move a two-dimensional

00:04:07.739 --> 00:04:12.849
slice through the third dimension, we can
see the spheres are all there after all.

00:04:12.849 --> 00:04:17.079
This model of the fourth dimension is more
than something the developer made up.

00:04:17.079 --> 00:04:22.360
These shapes have long been researched in
geometry, and objects like tesseracts, essentially

00:04:22.360 --> 00:04:25.350
4D cubes, are well-documented structures.

00:04:25.350 --> 00:04:29.810
Now, some of you might be thinking, ‘wait,
the fourth dimension is time.’

00:04:29.810 --> 00:04:32.500
Hold onto that comment, we’re going to get
to it.

00:04:32.500 --> 00:04:38.730
For now, there’s lots of cool stuff you
can make in 4D, like this 600-sided die, or

00:04:38.730 --> 00:04:43.450
fourth-dimensional abacuses, which really
makes me feel like a baby fumbling around

00:04:43.450 --> 00:04:44.450
with a toy.

00:04:44.450 --> 00:04:47.520
Ever tried to fit a tesseract into a 4D hole?

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:48.680
It’s not easy.

00:04:48.680 --> 00:04:53.190
I’m convinced this is how USBs work — you
can’t plug them in on the first try because

00:04:53.190 --> 00:04:56.020
they’re 4-D objects — wake up people.

00:04:56.020 --> 00:05:01.940
Trying to think in 4D can be a humbling experience,
and can help you sympathize with 2D life.

00:05:01.940 --> 00:05:07.540
For example, let’s imagine a 2D entity is
trying to get this sphere out of this ring.

00:05:07.540 --> 00:05:11.890
From our 3D perspective, the solution is obvious,
just pull it forwards.

00:05:11.890 --> 00:05:17.310
But to a 2D entity, disconnecting the two
shapes would seem completely impossible, and

00:05:17.310 --> 00:05:19.380
our solution would look like magic.

00:05:19.380 --> 00:05:23.210
But we’d have similar difficulties with
4D objects.

00:05:23.210 --> 00:05:27.490
These two rings seem like they could never
be separated, but they actually have a gap

00:05:27.490 --> 00:05:32.000
we can’t perceive along the fourth dimension,
allowing them to come apart.

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:36.600
It drives home the point that to a fourth
dimensional lifeform, we might be like newborns,

00:05:36.600 --> 00:05:40.210
utterly perplexed by the most basic of their
shapes.

00:05:40.210 --> 00:05:48.900
“Struck by the evil prankster’s dimension
ray, space itself shatters around hawkman’s

00:05:48.900 --> 00:05:49.900
ship.”

00:05:49.900 --> 00:05:50.900
But shapes are one thing.

00:05:50.900 --> 00:05:56.460
What would it be like if a fourth dimensional
being pulled us entirely into their four-dimensional

00:05:56.460 --> 00:05:57.550
world?

00:05:57.550 --> 00:06:00.580
The answer might be the game Miegakure.

00:06:00.580 --> 00:06:05.470
Created by the same developer as 4D Toys,
Miegakure takes place in a world with four

00:06:05.470 --> 00:06:09.180
dimensions — though we can only perceive
a 3D slice of it.

00:06:09.180 --> 00:06:14.380
But Miegakure allows you to move your 3D slice
along the fourth dimension, revealing new

00:06:14.380 --> 00:06:17.470
sides to buildings and landscapes you couldn’t
see before.

00:06:17.470 --> 00:06:22.810
It can be quite trippy to look at, but this
ability allows you to solve puzzles in interesting

00:06:22.810 --> 00:06:23.810
ways.

00:06:23.810 --> 00:06:25.470
Say there’s a wall in front you.

00:06:25.470 --> 00:06:30.800
You can move your plane of 3D until you reach
a point in 4D space where the wall isn’t

00:06:30.800 --> 00:06:32.840
there, then walk right through.

00:06:32.840 --> 00:06:37.900
Now, despite being in development for a while,
the game unfortunately still isn’t available

00:06:37.900 --> 00:06:42.789
to play, for the understandable reason that
trying to build a four-dimensional world as

00:06:42.789 --> 00:06:45.720
a three dimensional being is quite challenging.

00:06:45.720 --> 00:06:49.990
“Great Galaxies, where are we?”

00:06:49.990 --> 00:06:52.550
“Location, Dimension 23.”

00:06:52.550 --> 00:06:54.259
“DiMenSiOn 23?”

00:06:54.259 --> 00:06:57.930
Another promising 4D game is 4D Miner.

00:06:57.930 --> 00:07:03.590
Created by youtuber Mashpoe, this game resembles
regular Minecraft at first, until you scroll

00:07:03.590 --> 00:07:10.040
with the mouse wheel and realize we’re once
again seeing a 3D slice of a much larger 4D

00:07:10.040 --> 00:07:11.040
landscape.

00:07:11.040 --> 00:07:13.780
Surviving in this world stretches your mind.

00:07:13.780 --> 00:07:17.901
Sometimes you’ll think you’re in an area
without trees, only to realize they’re all

00:07:17.901 --> 00:07:21.540
around you, just hidden elsewhere in the fourth
dimension.

00:07:21.540 --> 00:07:26.370
Other times, you think you’ve found a small
cave, only to realize it’s huge.

00:07:26.370 --> 00:07:31.470
But most challenging of all, is the fact you
aren’t alone in this world.

00:07:31.470 --> 00:07:37.190
Those hovering shapes belong to fourth dimensional
predators that see you as an easy meal.

00:07:37.190 --> 00:07:42.020
Called hyperspiders, their bodies extend into
the fourth dimension, and are considerably

00:07:42.020 --> 00:07:44.210
larger than we can fathom.

00:07:44.210 --> 00:07:48.780
Because of their fourth dimensional bodies,
hyperspiders are difficult to spot, and even

00:07:48.780 --> 00:07:50.370
harder to hide from.

00:07:50.370 --> 00:07:55.660
If you build a house to take shelter in, they’ll
often appear inside as if from nowhere.

00:07:55.660 --> 00:08:01.180
This is because as 4D predators, hyperspiders
can move through gaps that us 3D lifeforms

00:08:01.180 --> 00:08:02.590
can easily overlook.

00:08:02.590 --> 00:08:05.550
“Meanwhile, back on the world in our dimension.”

00:08:05.550 --> 00:08:07.759
“What are we going to do?”

00:08:07.759 --> 00:08:10.719
“I’m afraid gentlemen, we are at their
mercy.”

00:08:10.719 --> 00:08:14.430
There are other ways of simulating four-dimensional
lifeforms.

00:08:14.430 --> 00:08:20.460
4D Particle Life is a game that works just
like a 2D life simulator, except, of course,

00:08:20.460 --> 00:08:21.530
in 4D.

00:08:21.530 --> 00:08:26.810
The artificial life on screen right now might
look like simple particles, but every simulated

00:08:26.810 --> 00:08:30.080
cell has a much more complex 4D body.

00:08:30.080 --> 00:08:36.469
It’s interesting to think about how games
have gone from 2D, to 3D, to now, with a few

00:08:36.469 --> 00:08:37.469
games, 4D.

00:08:37.469 --> 00:08:42.180
Although side note: many classic 2D games
aren’t truly two dimensional — as characters

00:08:42.180 --> 00:08:47.560
can pass in front of and behind objects, so
their worlds contain at least some depth.

00:08:47.560 --> 00:08:52.080
What’s really weird is that when you play
a 4D game, you’re technically looking at

00:08:52.080 --> 00:08:56.120
a 3D slice of a 4D world rendered on a 2D
screen.

00:08:56.120 --> 00:09:00.670
So now that no one is confused, let’s talk
about time travel.

00:09:00.670 --> 00:09:07.340
“Now to switch on my time conveyer!”

00:09:07.340 --> 00:09:11.649
You might have heard that time is the 4th
dimension, which raises the possibility of

00:09:11.649 --> 00:09:14.180
4D life but, like, with time instead.

00:09:14.180 --> 00:09:19.710
Whether or not time is best classified a dimension
like the spatial ones has long been a subject

00:09:19.710 --> 00:09:22.410
of debate, but let’s run with the idea.

00:09:22.410 --> 00:09:24.830
After all, you do ‘move’ through time.

00:09:24.830 --> 00:09:30.100
All of us, right now, are constantly traveling
forward from the temporal position we were

00:09:30.100 --> 00:09:32.720
in moments ago, simply by existing.

00:09:32.720 --> 00:09:38.600
So, by some definitions, you’re already
a 4D lifeform, with three dimensions of space

00:09:38.600 --> 00:09:39.670
and one dimension of time.

00:09:39.670 --> 00:09:41.490
“What was that strange field we just passed
through?”

00:09:41.490 --> 00:09:45.470
“It must have been some sort of time barrier.

00:09:45.470 --> 00:09:47.590
Look, a water dinosaur!”

00:09:47.590 --> 00:09:52.779
The difficulty is, of course, that we can
only move through time in a single direction.

00:09:52.779 --> 00:09:58.209
We are constantly traveling forwards, and
we cannot go back—or even know where we’ll

00:09:58.209 --> 00:09:59.209
end up.

00:09:59.209 --> 00:10:03.430
If you could see your fourth-dimensional self
across time, stretching all the way from your

00:10:03.430 --> 00:10:08.240
first moments to your very last, it’s interesting
to consider how that would change what it

00:10:08.240 --> 00:10:09.620
means to be human.

00:10:09.620 --> 00:10:14.500
If, from the very beginning of your story,
you knew every moment that’s to come, would

00:10:14.500 --> 00:10:18.070
that even be a life as we understand it?

00:10:18.070 --> 00:10:23.600
One of the few sci-fi films to seriously consider
the implications of transcending linear time

00:10:23.600 --> 00:10:24.790
is Arrival.

00:10:24.790 --> 00:10:30.970
The aliens in the movie, called ‘heptapods,’
seem to experience time in a non-linear fashion,

00:10:30.970 --> 00:10:35.940
with every moment in their lives, including
their death, as close to the present as any

00:10:35.940 --> 00:10:36.940
other.

00:10:36.940 --> 00:10:40.930
We’re not talking about a lifeform that
can alter time (not yet, anyway), but rather

00:10:40.930 --> 00:10:45.200
entities that can exist at any point along
a predetermined path.

00:10:45.200 --> 00:10:50.010
And as the film progresses, the main human
character begins to perceive time the way

00:10:50.010 --> 00:10:51.250
the heptapods do.

00:10:51.250 --> 00:10:56.450
It’s an ability that proves both beneficial
and heartbreaking, as all the good and bad

00:10:56.450 --> 00:11:00.350
in her life become contemporary instances
in time.

00:11:00.350 --> 00:11:05.700
One of the hardest parts of time passing is
that the future holds many unexpected tragedies.

00:11:05.700 --> 00:11:10.290
But if you knew every tragic event in your
life before they happened, yet couldn’t

00:11:10.290 --> 00:11:13.920
change anything, would that really make things
easier?

00:11:13.920 --> 00:11:19.260
Another reflection on the melancholy of non-linear
time is the film Interstellar, particularly

00:11:19.260 --> 00:11:23.829
in the scene where the lead character falls
into a higher dimensional space within a wormhole,

00:11:23.829 --> 00:11:27.339
where they’re able to see into the past
and future.

00:11:27.339 --> 00:11:32.350
The strange realm is called a Tesseract, even
though it’s not a 4D-cube in the traditional,

00:11:32.350 --> 00:11:33.350
spatial sense.

00:11:33.350 --> 00:11:38.170
But like Arrival, Interstellar’s portrayal
of the fourth dimension is more of a thematic

00:11:38.170 --> 00:11:43.579
device than a physics lesson — a meditation
on time’s unchangeable nature.

00:11:43.579 --> 00:11:47.930
We all want to know where we’re going, but
if you knew where your ship was headed, but

00:11:47.930 --> 00:11:52.550
couldn’t change course, perhaps you’d
be better off keeping your eyes closed.

00:11:52.550 --> 00:11:57.720
“You are now in dimension 5, the 5th dimension.

00:11:57.720 --> 00:12:00.660
Give me the library of Gorr!”

00:12:00.660 --> 00:12:07.000
“It was lost when you pulled me into this
world of yours, buddy.”

00:12:07.000 --> 00:12:12.459
But what if you could steer the ship, moving
across timelines the same way a higher dimensional

00:12:12.459 --> 00:12:16.680
lifeform could move through space, would that
make you 5D Life?

00:12:16.680 --> 00:12:19.470
While you ponder that question, let’s play
some chess.

00:12:19.470 --> 00:12:24.350
And by that, I of course mean “5D Chess
with Multiverse Timetravel.”

00:12:24.350 --> 00:12:26.420
5D chess plays…

00:12:26.420 --> 00:12:28.160
basically the same as regular chess.

00:12:28.160 --> 00:12:32.700
With the small exception that if a game just
isn’t going your way, you can send one of

00:12:32.700 --> 00:12:37.630
your pieces backwards through time, creating
a new branch reality where things can unfold

00:12:37.630 --> 00:12:38.630
differently.

00:12:38.630 --> 00:12:43.350
And you can do this as many times as you like,
meaning you have to avoid checkmate across

00:12:43.350 --> 00:12:46.020
multiple different parallel realities.

00:12:46.020 --> 00:12:47.530
You with me so far?

00:12:47.530 --> 00:12:51.490
While playing, you can take pieces out from
fifteen moves ago.

00:12:51.490 --> 00:12:56.390
You can send your king through time to another
reality, erasing your king from the original

00:12:56.390 --> 00:13:00.220
timeline and making it impossible for you
to lose in that universe.

00:13:00.220 --> 00:13:05.050
There’s a strategy called ‘Jurassic Rook,’
where you send a rook or ‘castle’ all

00:13:05.050 --> 00:13:09.480
the way back to the beginning of the game,
effectively resetting the timeline to its

00:13:09.480 --> 00:13:13.710
original state like you’re in Back to the
Future — it’s crazy, and can leave your

00:13:13.710 --> 00:13:15.050
mind completely shattered.

00:13:15.050 --> 00:13:21.899
“I just saw a trillion different realities
folding on to each other like thin sheets

00:13:21.899 --> 00:13:24.589
of metal forming a single blade.”

00:13:24.589 --> 00:13:27.050
“Yeah, yeah, the time knife, we’ve all
seen it.”

00:13:27.050 --> 00:13:33.100
But what’s potentially even crazier is imagining
how fifth-dimensional entities would hypothetically

00:13:33.100 --> 00:13:35.089
function in an ecosystem.

00:13:35.089 --> 00:13:40.430
Say you’re a 5D organism, aware of yourself
across multiple timelines.

00:13:40.430 --> 00:13:44.060
If you’re caught by a predator in one reality,
it doesn’t matter.

00:13:44.060 --> 00:13:47.589
In another point in another timeline, you’re
still doing fine.

00:13:47.589 --> 00:13:53.350
Or, you could go full Jurassic Rook and take
out a past version of that predator when they’re

00:13:53.350 --> 00:13:57.660
little, or better yet, take out their great
grandparent so they’re never born.

00:13:57.660 --> 00:14:02.889
It’s mind-boggling to think about a whole
speculative ecosystem of 5D species trying

00:14:02.889 --> 00:14:07.830
to out-juke each other across multiple timelines,
like they are Skynet from the Terminator.

00:14:07.830 --> 00:14:13.740
And to be clear, this vision of the 5th dimension
is beyond speculative and hardly the most

00:14:13.740 --> 00:14:14.880
scientific…

00:14:14.880 --> 00:14:18.550
but while we’re here, can we push things
even further?

00:14:18.550 --> 00:14:28.160
“This little trinket just sent you into
the 6th dimension, superman.

00:14:28.160 --> 00:14:31.660
The dimension behind mirrors.”

00:14:31.660 --> 00:14:36.050
Branching off into alternate realities from
a single timeline is one thing, but what if

00:14:36.050 --> 00:14:40.490
you could explore completely new timelines
with completely new rules?

00:14:40.490 --> 00:14:45.540
This multiverse model of reality has become
a popular one in recent years, with more and

00:14:45.540 --> 00:14:50.060
more pieces of media exploring stories where
divergent timelines collide.

00:14:50.060 --> 00:14:54.279
It’s a concept that doesn’t really line
up with the multi-dimensional theories of

00:14:54.279 --> 00:14:59.930
quantum physics, but let’s not get into
all that, this video is already plenty confusing.

00:14:59.930 --> 00:15:05.190
The most relevant piece of multiverse media
for our purpose of visualizing higher dimensional

00:15:05.190 --> 00:15:09.010
life is probably the film Everything, Everywhere,
All at Once.

00:15:09.010 --> 00:15:13.850
It’s a story in which various characters
can perceive and even merge with other versions

00:15:13.850 --> 00:15:16.710
of themselves from across alternate timelines.

00:15:16.710 --> 00:15:21.740
An interesting byproduct of this is that as
humans with brains only mean to perceive a

00:15:21.740 --> 00:15:27.100
single reality at a time, focusing on one
reality often means a version of the characters

00:15:27.100 --> 00:15:29.300
in another reality suffers.

00:15:29.300 --> 00:15:33.880
Like Arrival and Interstellar, the film’s
dimension-bending sequences are largely a

00:15:33.880 --> 00:15:36.140
vehicle for themes and emotional beats.

00:15:36.140 --> 00:15:40.580
It’s not just a movie about what would happen
if humans had hot-dogs for fingers.

00:15:40.580 --> 00:15:45.089
Well, it is a movie about that — but it’s
also a reflection on meaning in the face of

00:15:45.089 --> 00:15:46.089
infinity.

00:15:46.089 --> 00:15:50.430
In the context of limitless realities, what
is the worth of a single moment?

00:15:50.430 --> 00:15:55.160
It’s a question that feels particularly
relevant to life in the age of the internet.

00:15:55.160 --> 00:16:00.410
We can download and experience all these different
dimensional simulations, and it’s great!

00:16:00.410 --> 00:16:02.950
But it can also be overwhelming.

00:16:02.950 --> 00:16:07.970
In one of the film’s most stunning scenes,
the main character embodies all possible versions

00:16:07.970 --> 00:16:13.060
of herself across every conceivable reality
— completely losing her identity to the

00:16:13.060 --> 00:16:15.330
void of unlimited stimulation.

00:16:15.330 --> 00:16:20.380
Everything, Everywhere, All at Once is the
kind of narrative that can make you appreciate

00:16:20.380 --> 00:16:26.750
being here, now — an ordinary lifeform with
a reasonable number of perceivable dimensions.

00:16:26.750 --> 00:16:32.399
“My readings indicate we are not in time
and space as we understand it.

00:16:32.399 --> 00:16:36.829
Apparently, the natural laws of our universe
don’t operate here.”

00:16:36.829 --> 00:16:41.870
In terms of higher temporal dimensions, there’s
no real place to go after you’ve encompassed

00:16:41.870 --> 00:16:45.529
every point in time in every possible timeline.

00:16:45.529 --> 00:16:50.540
But if we leave time and return to spatial
dimensions, we’ve got room to grow.

00:16:50.540 --> 00:16:56.230
You now know what a 4D cube looks like, but
how about a 5D cube — with an extra dimension

00:16:56.230 --> 00:16:57.900
of space instead of time.

00:16:57.900 --> 00:16:59.980
Or a 10D cube?

00:16:59.980 --> 00:17:04.750
Yes, all these points and lines represent
a single 10-dimensional hypercube, flatted

00:17:04.750 --> 00:17:07.720
out in a way that we can perceive.

00:17:07.720 --> 00:17:12.549
You can really keep adding additional spatial
dimensions until things get exponentially

00:17:12.549 --> 00:17:18.200
out of control, with the monstrosity that’s
currently on screen a 16-dimensional hypercube.

00:17:18.200 --> 00:17:23.699
Unsurprisingly, there’s not a lot of media
that tries to explore life with these types

00:17:23.699 --> 00:17:25.080
of dimensions.

00:17:25.080 --> 00:17:31.030
The closest example probably comes from Douglass
Adams’s The Hitchhiker's Guide to the Galaxy.

00:17:31.030 --> 00:17:36.850
In this series, what we know as mice are revealed
to merely be small protrusions into our dimension

00:17:36.850 --> 00:17:39.280
belonging to vast, higher-dimensional beings.

00:17:39.280 --> 00:17:44.340
It’s a comedic concept that riffs on the
trope of rats and mazes.

00:17:44.340 --> 00:17:49.850
Much like how from our top-down perspective
a two-dimensional maze is easy to solve, mice

00:17:49.850 --> 00:17:55.260
in Hitchhiker's Guide find our mazes absurdly
simple — which might hold true for any higher

00:17:55.260 --> 00:17:56.630
dimensional being.

00:17:56.630 --> 00:18:01.150
“You can hide nothing from me.

00:18:01.150 --> 00:18:03.410
My x-ray intelligence.”

00:18:03.410 --> 00:18:08.240
We could also head in the other direction
and imagine a one-dimensional game — a concept

00:18:08.240 --> 00:18:11.930
the same creator who made 4D Miner has a great
take on.

00:18:11.930 --> 00:18:16.780
The game is technically a three-dimensional
interpretation of a one-dimension field of

00:18:16.780 --> 00:18:21.530
vision, as a completely 1D-game would just
be an impossibly thin line.

00:18:21.530 --> 00:18:26.240
Playing the simulation is kind of like living
in a world made solely of barcodes — although

00:18:26.240 --> 00:18:31.240
with practice, you can actually get better
at navigating the strange environment.

00:18:31.240 --> 00:18:33.809
Or how about a zero-dimensional game, which…

00:18:33.809 --> 00:18:38.929
I guess mathematically wouldn’t be a line
but just a single point — okay we’ve pushed

00:18:38.929 --> 00:18:41.570
things too far.

00:18:41.570 --> 00:18:46.850
In the end, imagining versions of reality
with a different number of spatial or temporal

00:18:46.850 --> 00:18:49.070
dimensions can be challenging.

00:18:49.070 --> 00:18:54.080
But there’s so many interesting things you
can experience by widening your perspective.

00:18:54.080 --> 00:18:58.570
Some forms of media might make you wish for
extra dimensions, while others will make you

00:18:58.570 --> 00:19:00.460
appreciate what you have.

00:19:00.460 --> 00:19:02.270
It all depends on your point of view.

00:19:02.270 --> 00:19:04.960
“We will not live in a two-dimensional world.

00:19:04.960 --> 00:19:09.000
We won’t go in one direction, or zig where
we can zag.

00:19:09.000 --> 00:19:10.679
We will walk through walls.

00:19:10.679 --> 00:19:12.370
We will not be confined.”

00:19:12.370 --> 00:19:16.549
There are plenty of other games and pieces
of media that experiment with dimensions of

00:19:16.549 --> 00:19:20.830
space and time in their own way that I didn’t
get the chance to talk about.

00:19:20.830 --> 00:19:25.250
If you found this subject thought-provoking,
there are links to all the games and simulations

00:19:25.250 --> 00:19:30.190
discussed in this video down below, with more
information on all the incredible people who

00:19:30.190 --> 00:19:34.309
are building worlds in higher and lower dimensions.

00:19:34.309 --> 00:19:36.750
As always, thanks for watching.

00:19:36.750 --> 00:19:41.490
If you enjoyed this entry, please lend your
support by liking, subscribing, and hitting

00:19:41.490 --> 00:19:46.010
the notification icon to stay up to date on
all things Curious.

00:19:46.010 --> 00:19:54.890
See you in the next video.

